// Exercises 8, Ex 8(2) | statement, logical-connectives | difficulty 1
::ex8-8-2::[html]Let A \= "I study logic", B \= "I will be an engineer". Which formula represents\: "I study logic but I will not be an engineer"? {
    ~A ∧ B#Incorrect
    =A ∧ ¬B#Correct
    ~¬A ∧ ¬B#Incorrect
    ~A → ¬B#Incorrect
}

// Exercises 8, Ex 8(5) | statement, logical-connectives | difficulty 1
::ex8-8-5::[html]Let A \= "I study logic", B \= "I will be an engineer". Which formula represents\: "If I study logic, then I will be an engineer"? {
    ~A ∨ B#Incorrect
    ~B → A#Incorrect
    =A → B#Correct
    ~A ↔ B#Incorrect
}

// Exercises 1, Ex 4/8a | set-operations | difficulty 1
::ex1-4-def::[html]What is the symmetric difference A Δ B equal to? {
    ~(A\\B) ∪ (B\\A)#Incorrect
    ~(A ∪ B)\\(A ∩ B)#Incorrect
    =Both — they are always equal#Correct
    ~Neither of these#Incorrect
}

// Exercises 1, Ex 5(1) | set-operations | difficulty 2
::ex1-5-comp::[html]Let A \= \{a, b, 1\}, B \= \{a, 1, 2\}, C \= \{1, 2\}. What is (A ∪ B)\\C ? {
    =\{a, b\}#Correct
    ~\{a\}#Incorrect
    ~\{1, 2\}#Incorrect
    ~\{a, b, 1, 2\}#Incorrect
}

